# Code owners — who OWNS each surface. This file is documentation, not a merge gate.
#
# "Require review from Code Owners" is deliberately OFF on this repo, so no rule below can
# fire and nothing here blocks a merge on its own. The required approving review count is
# separately set to 0, for a related but distinct reason: a sole code owner cannot approve
# their own pull request, so any nonzero count would deadlock every merge.
#
# What main's protection actually enforces (checked live, not assumed, read the setting
# itself with `gh api repos/kphutt/gdmutant/branches/main/protection`): a pull request is
# required, eight status checks must pass (`Workflow security (zizmor)`, `Verify (ubuntu-24.04)`,
# `Verify (windows-2025)`, `Secret scan (gitleaks)`, both `Self-test` (real Godot) jobs, and the
# two Socket Security GitHub App checks, `Socket Security: Project Report` and `Socket Security:
# Pull Request Alerts`), the branch must be up to date with main before merging, admin enforcement
# is ON (an admin cannot bypass any of this by pushing straight to main), and force-push and
# deletion are blocked. (A test in `tests/test_harden_github.py` asserts this file's stated
# count equals `len(harden_github.all_required_contexts())`, so this comment can't drift
# silently again the way it already did once.)
#
# ci.yml runs automatically on every pull request and push to main (restored 2026-08-04, ahead
# of going public, see docs/decisions/0012-merge-time-local-ship-time-cloud.md's "Correction
# (2026-08-04)"), so lint, types, tests, and the Godot self-tests all genuinely block a merge now,
# not just the workflow-security check. Local hooks (.pre-commit-config.yaml's pre-push stage,
# installed per clone via `pre-commit install --hook-type pre-commit --hook-type pre-push`) are
# the fast, optional, catches-it-earlier layer now, not the only gate a clone without them would
# be missing. Socket Security is a GitHub App, not a workflow job, so it reports independently of
# ci.yml; `scripts/harden_github.py`'s `REQUIRED_APP_CHECKS` is the config-as-code source for
# those two contexts, the same way `REQUIRED_JOBS` is for the workflow-derived six.
#
# The one check nothing above can skip is still publish.yml's release-time job, which re-runs
# verify on Linux and Windows, the license check, and both Godot self-tests live, on the exact
# commit being shipped. That stays true regardless of branch-protection settings, which an admin
# could in principle change. Merging is now mechanically enforced too, but shipping is the one
# gate that can never be turned off.
#
# So read the list below as "read this carefully before merging it yourself", not as the only
# thing standing between a bad change and main. Sensitive surfaces = the places where tests can't
# save you (config, CI, secrets, self-executing code).

# Default owner for everything.
* @kphutt

# --- Sensitive paths: read carefully before merging ------------------------
# CI / automation / anything self-executing on a runner.
/.github/        @kphutt
/scripts/        @kphutt
# Toolchain + supply-chain surfaces.
/pyproject.toml  @kphutt
/uv.lock         @kphutt
/.gitleaks.toml  @kphutt

# --- Engine surfaces -------------------------------------------------------
# The mutation-operator catalog + the GDScript adapter are the tool's real
# technical risk (a wrong mutant = a silently wrong survivor report). Read every
# change here carefully before merging.
/gdmutant/engine/            @kphutt
/gdmutant/engine/operators/  @kphutt
/gdmutant/adapters/          @kphutt
/gdmutant/cli.py             @kphutt
